<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Diffusion |</title><link>https://kgrosero.github.io/tags/diffusion/</link><atom:link href="https://kgrosero.github.io/tags/diffusion/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><description>Diffusion</description><generator>HugoBlox Kit (https://hugoblox.com)</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Wed, 01 Oct 2025 00:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><image><url>https://kgrosero.github.io/media/icon_hu_cd51c91342a84ed6.png</url><title>Diffusion</title><link>https://kgrosero.github.io/tags/diffusion/</link></image><item><title>Anonymizing Faces, Preserving Anomalies</title><link>https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/</link><pubDate>Wed, 01 Oct 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/</guid><description>&lt;p class="text-xl font-semibold"&gt;Deep-Learning Based Privacy Protection for Data of Patients with Cleft Lip&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Karen Rosero&lt;/strong&gt;, Lucas M. Harrison, Alex A. Kane, Carlos Busso, Rami R. Hallac&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;15th International Congress on Cleft Lip/Palate and Related Craniofacial Anomalies (CLEFT 2025), Kyoto, Japan&lt;/em&gt;&lt;br&gt;
In collaboration with UT Southwestern Medical Center and Children&amp;rsquo;s Health.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id="why-anonymize-clinical-photos"&gt;Why anonymize clinical photos?&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Facial photographs support treatment planning for patients with cleft lip: lip marking, assessment of surgical outcomes, facial symmetry analysis, and automatic detection of lip abnormalities. Using facial images in medical contexts also raises critical ethical and legal concerns about patients&amp;rsquo; privacy, which limits how these images can be shared for research and education.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;figure class="kr-figure"&gt;&lt;img src="https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/uses_hu_95e0f7c975e8143a.webp" srcset="https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/uses_hu_289d0aed52bcfc21.webp 800w, https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/uses_hu_95e0f7c975e8143a.webp 1200w, https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/uses_hu_e7c078342c00a47d.webp 1600w" sizes="(max-width: 1000px) 100vw, 960px" alt="Facial photographs support lip marking, surgical outcome assessment, facial symmetry analysis and lip abnormality detection." loading="lazy" decoding="async"&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id="traditional-anonymization-falls-short"&gt;Traditional anonymization falls short&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Black bars over the eyes, pixelation and blurring are the usual ways to de-identify a face. Generative AI can reconstruct a highly similar face from any of them, so they do not reliably protect the patient.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;figure class="kr-figure"&gt;&lt;img src="https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/traditional_hu_472270168632adfd.webp" srcset="https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/traditional_hu_ab1cc0751d83cab8.webp 800w, https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/traditional_hu_472270168632adfd.webp 1200w, https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/traditional_hu_556d6209c99490c1.webp 1600w" sizes="(max-width: 1000px) 100vw, 960px" alt="Black bar over the eyes, face pixelation and face blurring." loading="lazy" decoding="async"&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;figure class="kr-figure"&gt;&lt;img src="https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/reconstruction_hu_4211409d765b38d2.webp" srcset="https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/reconstruction_hu_1ebe25bc2dfd15ff.webp 800w, https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/reconstruction_hu_4211409d765b38d2.webp 1200w, https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/reconstruction_hu_4b50dc5973f5d7b9.webp 1600w" sizes="(max-width: 1000px) 100vw, 960px" alt="Generative AI can reconstruct a highly similar face from each traditional anonymization." loading="lazy" decoding="async"&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id="our-approach-replace-the-face-keep-the-anomaly"&gt;Our approach: replace the face, keep the anomaly&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We preserve the orofacial anatomy of the patient and use a generative AI system to synthesize the rest of the face. The result is a realistic portrait of a person who does not exist, with the clinically relevant region left exactly as it was.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;figure class="kr-figure"&gt;&lt;img src="https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/proposal_hu_1c899400bda18bfe.webp" srcset="https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/proposal_hu_aa604061c919b1a3.webp 800w, https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/proposal_hu_1c899400bda18bfe.webp 1200w, https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/proposal_hu_3bcf468e8dcccd2d.webp 1600w" sizes="(max-width: 1000px) 100vw, 960px" alt="The original orofacial region is kept and a generative model synthesizes the rest of the face." loading="lazy" decoding="async"&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Landmarks.&lt;/strong&gt; Facial landmarks are detected automatically on the original image, using our
(FG 2024).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mask.&lt;/strong&gt; A mask isolates the orofacial region of interest. It can be adjusted manually or personalized for other facial regions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Synthesis.&lt;/strong&gt; A diffusion model, guided by the orofacial region and a text prompt (for example, &lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;light skin girl, green eyes&amp;rdquo;&lt;/em&gt;), generates the new face by progressive denoising. The region of interest stays unaltered, and the text controls the appearance of the synthetic face.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;figure class="kr-figure"&gt;&lt;img src="https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/methodology_hu_dd5465f4c244c284.webp" srcset="https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/methodology_hu_276a650600069042.webp 800w, https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/methodology_hu_dd5465f4c244c284.webp 1200w, https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/methodology_hu_4a468e15f4fe8e39.webp 1600w" sizes="(max-width: 1000px) 100vw, 960px" alt="Original image with facial landmarks, and the mask for the orofacial area." loading="lazy" decoding="async"&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;figure class="kr-figure"&gt;&lt;img src="https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/diffusion_hu_28548aac27a5de0.webp" srcset="https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/diffusion_hu_87377f2cab5337e1.webp 800w, https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/diffusion_hu_28548aac27a5de0.webp 1200w, https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/diffusion_hu_7403cd9b77bbef9c.webp 1600w" sizes="(max-width: 1000px) 100vw, 960px" alt="Image synthesis starts from noise and progressively denoises into a face around the preserved region." loading="lazy" decoding="async"&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id="results"&gt;Results&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The approach generalizes to both pre- and post-operative cleft lip cases and produces realistic, anatomically consistent synthetic faces.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;figure class="kr-figure"&gt;&lt;img src="https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/results_hu_47c0e4897620585d.webp" srcset="https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/results_hu_376bbe79cdbb2e86.webp 800w, https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/results_hu_47c0e4897620585d.webp 1200w, https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/results_hu_fe109f87b5cc40bd.webp 1600w" sizes="(max-width: 1000px) 100vw, 960px" alt="Generated faces for pre-operative and post-operative cases. All faces shown are generated." loading="lazy" decoding="async"&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We anonymized &lt;strong&gt;140 images&lt;/strong&gt; of patients with cleft lip, evenly distributed between pre- and post-operative cases, and asked two questions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Is the generated face recognized as the same person?&lt;/strong&gt; Each original image was compared with 8 synthesized variants using a deep-learning identity verification tool, for a total of &lt;strong&gt;1,120 generated images&lt;/strong&gt;. &lt;strong&gt;98.9%&lt;/strong&gt; were classified as distinct individuals. The remaining 1.1% were flagged as potential matches, all with low confidence (below 70%).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;figure class="kr-figure"&gt;&lt;img src="https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/reidentification_hu_c12c991524570db0.webp" srcset="https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/reidentification_hu_1390f3c2c5519c17.webp 800w, https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/reidentification_hu_c12c991524570db0.webp 1200w, https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/reidentification_hu_b317ee3590ac3aef.webp 1600w" sizes="(max-width: 1000px) 100vw, 960px" alt="An original image (blurred) and several of its synthesized variants." loading="lazy" decoding="async"&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Is the orofacial region preserved?&lt;/strong&gt; A clinical evaluator rated 50 images on a 1–5 scale, where 5 means high anatomical preservation after anonymization. &lt;strong&gt;48 images received a 5&lt;/strong&gt; and the other 2 received a 4.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;figure class="kr-figure"&gt;&lt;img src="https://kgrosero.github.io/projects/anonymizing-faces/preserved_hu_d8c06b2e3d89b83d.webp" alt="Generated faces that preserve the orofacial anatomy of the original patients." loading="lazy" decoding="async"&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id="takeaways"&gt;Takeaways&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A privacy-preserving method for facial portraits of patients with cleft lip.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It retains the clinical features that matter for evaluation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It makes it possible to build and share more diverse clinical datasets.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Images on this page are illustrations, images generated by our method, or original images blurred as in the conference presentation.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
